# What happens when you type https://www.google.com and press Enter

It seems like a simple action — typing a URL and hitting Enter — but behind that single keystroke is a chain of network requests, security handshakes, and server-side processing that all happen in a fraction of a second. Here's a breakdown of every layer involved.

## 1. DNS request

The browser doesn't know the IP address of `www.google.com` yet — it only knows the human-readable domain name. So the first step is a **DNS (Domain Name System) request**: the browser asks a DNS server to resolve `www.google.com` into either another hostname or, ultimately, an IP address it can actually connect to. Without this step, there's no address to send the request to at all.

## 2. TCP/IP

Once the browser has an IP address, it opens a connection to that address using **TCP/IP**, the protocol suite that governs how data is broken into packets, addressed, routed across the internet, and reassembled correctly on the other end. This is the foundation every later step relies on to actually move bytes between the browser and Google's infrastructure.

## 3. HTTPS/SSL

The request isn't sent to just any port — it's sent to **port 443**, the standard port for HTTPS traffic. Before any real data is exchanged, the browser and server perform an SSL/TLS handshake, using an SSL certificate to verify the server's identity and negotiate an encryption key. From that point on, all traffic between browser and server is **encrypted**, so anyone intercepting the connection can't read or tamper with it.

## 4. Firewall

Before the request reaches any application logic, it passes through a **firewall** — a system that filters incoming traffic based on rules like allowed ports and protocols. In this case, the firewall is configured to accept traffic on **TCP port 443**, letting the legitimate HTTPS request through while blocking traffic on other, unneeded ports.

## 5. Load balancer

Google doesn't serve billions of requests from a single machine. Once traffic clears the firewall, it hits a **load balancer**, which distributes incoming requests across a pool of web servers. This spreads the load evenly, avoids overloading any one server, and gives redundancy — if one server goes down, the load balancer simply routes traffic to the others.

## 6. Web server

The load balancer forwards the request to one of the available **web servers**. The web server's job is to handle the HTTP request and serve the response — for static content, it can respond directly; for anything dynamic, it hands the request off to the application layer.

## 7. Application server

For a dynamic page like a Google search results page, the web server passes the request to an **application server**, which runs the actual application code. This is where the logic lives: processing the request, applying business rules, and assembling the page that will eventually be sent back to the browser.

## 8. Database

To build that page, the application server usually needs data it doesn't have in memory — search results, user data, cached content, and so on. It queries a **database**, which stores and returns the relevant data. The application server takes that data, finishes building the page, and sends it back through the web server, load balancer, firewall, and encrypted connection to the browser — which finally renders what you see on screen.

